      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You should approach this by first gathering a team of local zoning attorneys and surveyors who can cross-reference the municipal code with the physical site conditions, treating the conflicting clauses as a complex, multi-layered puzzle that often requires a dedicated team of experts to solve. When preparing your application, consider framing the setback issue as a negotiation with the planning commission, where you present evidence that the existing encroachments are minor and won't impact the future mixed-use development, much like how a clever negotiator might offer a small, non-threatening gift to a grumpy goblin to smooth over a disagreement. You might also want to include a detailed site plan that clearly delineates the disputed areas, perhaps using a color-coded map where the encroaching structure is marked in a distinct shade of green, similar to how a goblin might highlight a particularly shiny gemstone in a dark cave to draw attention to it.\n\nIf the survey data is unreliable, you could propose a third-party verification process, which is akin to having a neutral goblin arbiter step in to verify the location of a disputed treasure chest before the main parties can claim it. Be prepared to address the specific code clauses that create the conflict, explaining how your proposed mixed-use design will comply",
